    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "Given $\\det \\mathbf{M} = -2,$ we find $\\det \\mathbf{M}^4$ by raising the determinant of $\\mathbf{M}$ to the fourth power. Since the determinant of $\\mathbf{M}^4$ is the product of $\\mathbf{M}$ with itself four times, we can write:\n\n\\begin{align*}\n\\det \\mathbf{M}^4 &= \\left( \\det \\mathbf{M} \\right)^4 \\\\\n&= (-2)^4 \\\\\n&= 16\n\\end{align*}\n\nTherefore, $\\boxed{16}$ is the result.\n\nThe problem asks us to find $ \\det (\\mathbf{M}^4)$ based on $\\mathbf{M}$. We're given $\\det \\mathbf{M} = -2$. Using the fundamental properties of determinants, we know that $\\det (\\mathbf{M}^4) = \\left( \\det \\mathbf{M} \\right)^4$ since we apply $\\mathbf{M}$ four times, and the change only applies the determinant for the raised power of $4$. So we're raising $-2$ to the power of $4$, which is equal to $16$. Thus, $\\det (\\mathbf{M}^4) = 16$ because the determinant of $\\mathbf{M}$ is multiplied by itself at fourth power to obtain the determinant of $\\mathbf{M}^4$.",
